public static String canTravelMaze(int n, int[] start, int[] end, int[][] paths) {
    // initialize a boolean variable to represent if travel is possible
    boolean canTravel = true;

    // check if starting and ending n-tersections are the same
    if (start[0] == end[0] && start[1] == end[1]) {
        // return "can be travelled" since starting and ending n-tersections are the same
        return "can be travelled";
    }

    // create a set to store visited n-tersections
    HashSet<Integer> visited = new HashSet<>();

    // add the starting n-tersection to the visited set
    visited.add(start);

    // create a queue to store n-tersections to be visited
    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    // enqueue the starting n-tersection
    queue.offer(start);

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        // dequeue an n-tersection
        int ntersection = queue.poll();

        // check if the n-tersection is the ending n-tersection
        if (ntersection == end) {
            // return "can be travelled" since the ending n-tersection has been visited
            return "can be travelled";
        }

        // find the adjacent n-tersections that are not in the visited set
        for (int path : paths[ntersection]) {
            if (!visited.contains(path)) {
                // add the adjacent n-tersection to the visited set
                visited.add(path);

                // enqueue the adjacent n-tersection
                queue.offer(path);
            }
        }
    }

    // return "cannot be travelled" since the ending n-tersection has not been visited
    return "cannot be travelled";
}
```
This code uses a breadth-first search to traverse the n-teresting space and determine if it is possible to travel from the starting n-tersection to the ending n-tersection. The `visited` set is used to keep track of which n-tersections have been visited, and the `queue` is used to store the n-tersections that need to be visited. The `while` loop iterates until an n-tersection in the queue is the ending n-tersection, or until the queue is empty. If the ending n-tersection has been visited, the method returns "can be travelled". Otherwise, it returns "cannot be travelled".

